rison lists (Outcome vs. Identity-based).\\n* **Icons & Vector Props:** Medals, trophies, bicycles, beds, mobile phones, jars with paperclips, books, cigarette illustrations, and social media app icons.\\n* **Real World Inserts / Photos:** Occasional cut-out cutout photographs of key personalities (Dave Brailsford, László Polgár, Judit Polgár, Roger Federer) and the actual physical book *Atomic Habits* (at the beginning and end).\\n* **Backgrounds:** Clean, seamless, plain white canvas throughout the entire presentation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### **3. Scene Structure & Visual Flow**\\n* **Frame Structure:** The canvas is organized into localized clusters of ideas. The hand draws one element, writes supporting labels next to it, and connects them with arrows or dividing lines.\\n* **Screen Divisions:** Frequently splits the white canvas into 2-column or 4-grid comparative sections (e.g., Good Habits vs. Bad Habits).\\n* **Transitions & Camera Movement:** \\n  * Hard canvas pans/wipes to new empty sections of the whiteboard once a topic is finished.\\n  * Mostly static framing while individual assets are being drawn, followed by smooth digital pans/slides to the next illustration.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### **4. Software & Tools to Recreate This Style**\\nTo produce this exact visual aesthetic, a creator would use:\\n* **Dedicated Whiteboard Software:**\\n  * **VideoScribe (Sparkol)** *(Most likely tool used here)*\\n  * **Doodly**\\n  * **Explaindio** or **Vyond** (Whiteboard mode)\\n* **Custom Graphics/Vector Libraries:**\\n  * **Adobe Illustrator / Inkscape:** For creating custom `.SVG` vector paths so the software hand traces lines accurately.\\n  * Stock SVG/Vector sites (Freepik, Envato Elements, FlatIcon, The Noun Project).\\n* **Video Editing & Compositing:**\\n  * **Adobe Premiere Pro / DaVinci Resolve / Final Cut Pro:** For timing animations to voiceover, speed-ramping, audio sync, and adding live-action book footage at the end.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### **5. Narration Style**\\n* **Language:** Hindi.\\n* **Pacing:** Moderate, articulate, and well-structured; matches the drawing speed perfectly.\\n* **Tone:** Educational, inspiring, authoritative, yet approachable (standard self-improvement/book summary style).\\n* **Audio Layering:** Clear, dry studio voiceover with a subtle, upbeat acoustic/corporate ambient background music track running continuously at low volume.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### **6. Stock Footage vs. Custom Animation**\\n* **Majority (~90%):** Standard whiteboard vector assets (pre-drawn SVG graphics animated by software hand-drawing engines).\\n* **Cut-outs (~5%):** Cut-out transparent PNG photos of real people and product covers.\\n* **Live Action Footage (~5%):** A brief live-action B-roll clip showing hands holding and showcasing the physical *Atomic Habits* paperback book at the outro (15:36–15:48).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### **Summary of the Production Recipe**\\n1. **Script & Voiceover:** Record and edit clear Hindi narration explaining chapter key takeaways.\\n2. **Storyboarding:** Map out metaphors (paperclips in jars, 4-step loops, 1% compound lines).\\n3. **Asset Gathering:** Collect or design SVG vector illustrations corresponding to each talking point.\\n4. **Animation:** Import SVGs into **VideoScribe**, set draw times, hand styles, and camera zoom paths synced to the VO track.\\n5. **Final Cut:** Export to a video editor to fine-tune pacing, add live-action book shots, and master background audio.\"},\"refunded\":false,\"message\":null,\"_credits\":{\"used\":0}}\nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":79,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T18:10:11Z","content":"I now have the complete video production analysis from vidIQ. Let me now compile this into a clear, direct answer.\n\nPriyanshu, here's the full breakdown from vidIQ's video watch analysis. The video is a **Hindi book summary of \"Atomic Habits\"** using classic whiteboard animation.\n\n---\n\n## What This Video Actually Uses\n\n### Animation Style: Classic Whiteboard Animation\n\nvidIQ confirmed the video uses a **simulated hand-drawing effect** — a 2D hand overlay holding a marker pen \"draws\" each illustration on a white canvas in real time. This is NOT After Effects or complex motion graphics. It's purpose-built whiteboard software.\n\n### The Exact Visual Elements\n\n| Element | Description | % of Video |\n|---------|-------------|-----------|\n| **Whiteboard drawings** | Pre-made SVG vector illustrations animated by the software's \"hand draw\" engine | ~90% |\n| **Text overlays** | Bold sans-serif keywords drawn in black/red/blue marker style | Continuous |\n| **Charts/infographics** | 4-quadrant diagrams, bell curves, comparison grids, arrow flows | Frequent |\n| **Photo cutouts** | PNG photos of real people (Dave Brailsford, Judit Polgár, etc.) placed on the canvas | ~5% |\n| **Live-action B-roll** | Brief clip of hands holding the physical book at the outro | ~5% |\n\n### The Software (What vidIQ Identified)\n\nThe most likely tool is **VideoScribe by Sparkol**. Here's why and what the alternatives are:\n\n| Tool | Price | Why It Fits |\n|------|-------|------------|\n| **VideoScribe (Sparkol)** | ~$15/month or $99/year | Most likely tool. Pre-built hand-drawing engine, SVG import, camera path control, huge asset library. This is the industry standard for this exact style. |\n| **Doodly** | $67 one-time (standard) | Budget alternative. Simpler, fewer features, but produces a similar look. Good for beginners. |\n| **Explaindio** | $67 one-time | Combines whiteboard + 2D animation. More flexible but steeper learning curve. |\n\n---\n\n## How to Recreate This — Step by Step\n\n### The Production Pipeline (Based on vidIQ's Analysis)\n\n```\nScript (2-3 hrs) → Voiceover (1 hr) → Storyboard (1 hr) → SVG Assets (1-2 hrs) → VideoScribe Animation (3-4 hrs) → Final Edit (1 hr)\n```\n\n**Total per video: ~10-12 hours for a 15-minute whiteboard explainer.** At 6 videos/month, that's 60-72 hours/month — doable as a part-time commitment.\n\n### Step 1: Script & Voiceover\n- Write the script in Hindi (or Hindi-English mix)\n- Record using a decent mic, or use **ElevenLabs** for a cloned/AI voice (if going faceless)\n- Length: 15-20 minutes of narration\n\n### Step 2: Storyboard\n- Map out which visual goes with which narration segment\n- Plan canvas sections: \"This part gets a 4-quadrant diagram, this part gets a character illustration\"\n\n### Step 3: Gather SVG Assets\n- **Free**: Flaticon, The Noun Project, Freepik (SVG vectors)\n- **Paid**: Envato Elements ($16.50/month — unlimited SVGs and illustrations)\n- **Custom**: Use **Inkscape** (free) or **Adobe Illustrator** to draw custom SVGs when needed\n\n### Step 4: Animate in VideoScribe\n- Import SVGs, set the draw order and timing\n- Sync to your voiceover track\n- Set camera pans between canvas sections\n- The software automatically animates the \"hand drawing\" effect\n\n### Step 5: Final Edit\n- Export from VideoScribe → import to **DaVinci Resolve** (free) or **Premiere Pro**\n- Add background music (Epidemic Sound, or use Suno to generate)\n- Add any photo cutouts or B-roll clips\n- Export at 1080p\n\n---\n\n## The 2026 AI-Accelerated Version\n\nHere's where it gets faster. You can cut that 10-12 hours down to 5-7 hours:\n\n| Step | Traditional | AI-Accelerated |\n|------|------------|---------------|\n| **Script** | Write manually (2-3 hrs) | Claude/ChatGPT draft → you edit (1 hr) |\n| **Voiceover** | Record yourself (1 hr) | ElevenLabs voice clone (15 min) |\n| **SVG assets** | Search stock sites (1-2 hrs) | AI image gen → vectorize with Vectorizer.io (30 min) |\n| **Animation** | VideoScribe manual timeline (3-4 hrs) | VideoScribe still manual (3-4 hrs) — no AI shortcut here yet |\n| **Music** | Search library (30 min) | Suno generates custom track (5 min) |\n| **Final edit** | Premiere/DaVinci (1 hr) | Same (1 hr) |\n| **TOTAL** | **10-12 hrs** | **5-7 hrs** |\n\n### Tools Stack (Complete)\n\n| Purpose | Tool | Cost |\n|---------|------|------|\n| Animation | **VideoScribe** | $99/year |\n| Script assistance | Claude/ChatGPT | Free-$20/month |\n| Voiceover | **ElevenLabs** | Free tier or $5/month |\n| SVG assets | Flaticon + The Noun Project | Free/freemium |\n| Video editing | **DaVinci Resolve** | Free |\n| Background music | **Suno** | Free tier |\n| Thumbnail | Canva or GPT Image | Free-$13/month |\n\n**Total cost: ~$15-35/month** to produce professional whiteboard explainers.\n\n---\n\n## The Honest Assessment: Does This Format Work for Your Goals?\n\nGoing back to what I told you in the previous analysis — whiteboard animation fits a specific category of topics well. And this video proves it: it's a **book summary** channel, not a geopolitics channel.\n\n**This format + your constraints (6 videos/month, 15-20 min, faceless) = a strong match for:**\n- Book summaries (like this video)\n- Business case studies\n- Self-improvement / psychology explainers\n- Economics / finance concepts\n- \"How things work\" educational content\n\n**It does NOT match geopolitics/military/political scandals** (needs maps, archival photos, cinematic graphics — not stickmen).\n\nIf you want to do topics like this video — book summaries, habit science, productivity, business stories — in Hindi with whiteboard animation, that's a proven format. This specific video has been accumulating views since June 2019 and is still getting ~30-40 VPH after 7 years. That's the definition of evergreen content.\n\nDo you want me to identify the specific channel this video belongs to and analyze their full content strategy and earnings?"}
